#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:-1
msgid ""
"Looking to optimize your documentation project for search engines? This "
"SEO guide will help your docs be better understood by both people and "
"crawlers as well as help you rank higher in search engine results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:2
msgid "How to do search engine optimization (SEO) for documentation projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:10
msgid ""
"This article explains how documentation can be optimized to appear in "
"search results, ultimately increasing traffic to your docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:13
msgid ""
"While you optimize your docs to make them more friendly for search engine"
" spiders/crawlers, it's important to keep in mind that your ultimate goal"
" is to make your docs more :term:`discoverable <discoverability>` for "
"your users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:17
msgid ""
"By following :ref:`our best practices for SEO <guides/technical-docs-seo-"
"guide:Best practices for documentation SEO>`, you can ensure that when a "
"user types a question into a search engine, they can get the answers from"
" your documentation in the search results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:23
msgid ""
"This guide isn't meant to be your only resource on SEO, and there's a lot"
" of SEO topics not covered here. For additional reading, please see the "
":ref:`external resources <guides/technical-docs-seo-guide:External "
"resources>` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:30
msgid "SEO basics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:32
msgid ""
"Search engines like Google and Bing crawl through the internet following "
"links in an attempt to understand and build an index of what various "
"pages and sites are about. This is called \"crawling\" or \"indexing\". "
"When a person sends a query to a search engine, the search engine "
"evaluates this index using a number of factors and attempts to return the"
" results most likely to answer that person's question."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:40
msgid ""
"How search engines \"rank\" sites based on a person's query is part of "
"their secret sauce. While some search engines publish the basics of their"
" algorithms (see Google's published details on PageRank), few search "
"engines give all of the details in an attempt to prevent users from "
"gaming the rankings with low value content which happens to rank well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:47
msgid ""
"Both `Google <https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/35769>`_ and "
"`Bing <https://www.bing.com/webmaster/help/webmaster-guidelines-"
"30fba23a>`_ publish a set of guidelines to help make sites easier to "
"understand for search engines and rank better. To summarize some of the "
"most important aspects as they apply to technical documentation, your "
"site should:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:53
msgid ""
"Use descriptive and accurate titles in the HTML ``<title>`` tag. For "
"Sphinx, the ``<title>`` comes from the first heading on the page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:55
msgid ""
"Ensure your URLs are descriptive. They are displayed in search results. "
"Sphinx uses the source filename without the file extension as the URL."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:57
msgid ""
"Make sure the words your readers would search for to find your site are "
"actually included on your pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:59
msgid "Avoid low content pages or pages with minimal original content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:60
msgid ""
"Avoid tactics that attempt to increase your search engine ranking without"
" actually improving content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:62
msgid ""
"Google specifically `warns about automatically generated content`_ "
"although this applies primarily to keyword stuffing and low value "
"content. High quality documentation generated from source code (eg. auto "
"generated API documentation) seems OK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:69
msgid ""
"While both Google and Bing discuss site performance as an important "
"factor in search result ranking, this guide is not going to discuss it in"
" detail. Most technical documentation that uses Sphinx or Read the Docs "
"generates static HTML and the performance is typically decent relative to"
" most of the internet."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:77
msgid "Best practices for documentation SEO"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:79
msgid ""
"Once a crawler or spider finds your site, it will follow links and "
"redirects in an attempt to find any and all pages on your site. While "
"there are a few ways to guide the search engine in its crawl for example "
"by using a :ref:`sitemap <seo_sitemap.xml>` or a :ref:`robots.txt file "
"<seo_robots.txt>` which we'll discuss shortly, the most important thing "
"is making sure the spider can follow links on your site and get to all "
"your pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:89
msgid "Avoid unlinked pages ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:91
msgid ""
"When building your documentation, you should ensure that pages aren't "
"*unlinked*, meaning that no other pages or navigation have a link to "
"them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:95
msgid ""
"Search engine crawlers will not discover pages that aren't linked from "
"somewhere else on your site."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:101
#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:149
msgid "Sphinx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:103
msgid ""
"Sphinx calls pages that don't have links to them \"orphans\" and will "
"throw a warning while building documentation that contains an orphan "
"unless the warning is silenced with the :ref:`orphan directive "
"<sphinx:metadata>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:107
msgid ""
"We recommend failing your builds whenever Sphinx warns you, using the "
"``fail_on_warnings`` option in :ref:`.readthedocs.yaml <config-"
"file/v2:sphinx>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:110
msgid "Here is an example of a warning of an unreferenced page::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:121
#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:162
msgid "MkDocs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:123
msgid ""
"MkDocs automatically includes all ``.md`` files in the main navigation "
"💯️. This makes sure that all files are discoverable by default, however "
"there are configurations that allow for unlinked files in various ways. "
"If you want to scan your documentation for unreferenced files and images,"
" a plugin like `mkdocs-unused-files`_ does the job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:134
msgid "Avoid uncrawlable content ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:136
msgid ""
"While typically this isn't a problem with technical documentation, try to"
" avoid content that is \"hidden\" from search engines. This includes "
"content hidden in images or videos which the crawler may not understand. "
"For example, if you do have a video in your docs, make sure the rest of "
"that page describes the content of the video."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:143
msgid ""
"When using images, make sure to set the image alt text or set a caption "
"on figures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:151
msgid ""
"For Sphinx, the image and figure directives support both alt texts and "
"captions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:164
msgid "The Markdown syntax defines an alt text for images:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:170
msgid ""
"Though HTML supports figures and captions, Markdown and MkDocs do not "
"have a built-in feature. Instead, you can use markdown extensions such as"
" `md-in-html`_ to allow the necessary HTML structures for including "
"figures:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:188
msgid "Redirects ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:190
msgid ""
"Redirects tell search engines when content has moved. For example, if "
"this guide moved from ``guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.html`` to "
"``guides/sphinx-seo-guide.html``, there will be a time period where "
"search engines will still have the old URL in their index and will still "
"be showing it to users. This is why it is important to update your own "
"links within your docs as well as redirecting. If the hostname moved from"
" docs.readthedocs.io to docs.readthedocs.org, this would be even more "
"important!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:197
msgid ""
"Read the Docs supports a few different kinds of :doc:`user defined "
"redirects </user-defined-redirects>` that should cover all the different "
"cases such as redirecting a certain page for all project versions, or "
"redirecting one version to another."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:207
msgid "Canonical URLs ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:209
msgid ""
"Anytime very similar content is hosted at multiple URLs, it is pretty "
"important to set a canonical URL. The canonical URL tells search engines "
"where the original version your documentation is even if you have "
"multiple versions on the internet (for example, incomplete translations "
"or deprecated versions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:215
msgid ""
"Read the Docs supports :doc:`setting the canonical URL </canonical-urls>`"
" if you are using a :doc:`custom domain </custom-domains>` in your "
"project's :menuselection:`Settings --> Domains`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:223
msgid "Use a robots.txt file ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:225
msgid ""
"A ``robots.txt`` file is readable by crawlers and lives at the root of "
"your site (eg. https://docs.readthedocs.com/robots.txt). It tells search "
"engines which pages to crawl or not to crawl and can allow you to control"
" how a search engine crawls your site. For example, you may want to "
"request that search engines :ref:`ignore unsupported versions of your "
"documentation <faq:How can I avoid search results having a deprecated "
"version of my docs?>` while keeping those docs online in case people need"
" them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:233
msgid ""
"By default, Read the Docs serves a ``robots.txt`` for you. To customize "
"this file, you can create a ``robots.txt`` file that is written to your "
"documentation root on your default branch/version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:237
msgid ""
"See the `Google's documentation on robots.txt "
"<https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/6062608>`_ for additional "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:243
msgid "Use a sitemap.xml file ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:245
msgid ""
"A sitemap is a file readable by crawlers that contains a list of pages "
"and other files on your site and some metadata or relationships about "
"them (eg. https://docs.readthedocs.com/sitemap.xml). A good sitemaps "
"provides information like how frequently a page or file is updated or any"
" alternate language versions of a page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:251
msgid ""
"Read the Docs generates a sitemap for you that contains the last time "
"your documentation was updated as well as links to active versions, "
"subprojects, and translations your project has. We have a small separate "
"guide on :doc:`sitemaps </reference/sitemaps>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:255
msgid ""
"See the `Google docs on building a sitemap "
"<https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/183668>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:259
msgid "Use meta tags ✅️"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:261
msgid ""
"Using a meta description allows you to customize how your pages look in "
"search engine result pages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:264
msgid ""
"Typically search engines will use the first few sentences of a page if no"
" meta description is provided. In Sphinx, you can customize your meta "
"description using the following RestructuredText:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:279
msgid "Google search engine results showing a customized meta description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:281
msgid ""
"Moz.com, an authority on search engine optimization, makes the following "
"suggestions for meta descriptions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:284
msgid ""
"Your meta description should have the most relevant content of the page. "
"A searcher should know whether they've found the right page from the "
"description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:286
msgid ""
"The meta description should be between 150-300 characters and it may be "
"truncated down to around 150 characters in some situations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:288
msgid "Meta descriptions are used for display but not for ranking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:290
msgid ""
"Search engines don't always use your customized meta description if they "
"think a snippet from the page is a better description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:294
msgid "Measure, iterate, & improve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:296
msgid ""
"Search engines (and soon, Read the Docs itself) can provide useful data "
"that you can use to improve your docs' ranking on search engines."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:301
msgid "Search engine feedback"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:303
msgid ""
"`Google Search Console <https://search.google.com/search-console>`_ and "
"`Bing Webmaster Tools <https://www.bing.com/webmaster/help/webmaster-"
"guidelines-30fba23a>`_ are tools for webmasters to get feedback about the"
" crawling of their sites (or docs in our case). Some of the most valuable"
" feedback these provide include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:309
msgid ""
"Google and Bing will show pages that were previously indexed that now "
"give a 404 (or more rarely a 500 or other status code). These will remain"
" in the index for some time but will eventually be removed. This is a "
"good opportunity to create a :ref:`redirect <seo_redirects>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:313
msgid "These tools will show any crawl issues with your documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:314
msgid ""
"Search Console and Webmaster Tools will highlight security issues found "
"or if Google or Bing took action against your site because they believe "
"it is spammy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:319
msgid "Analytics tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:321
msgid ""
"A tool like Google Analytics, along with our :doc:`integrated Read the "
"Docs analytics </traffic-analytics>`, can give you feedback about the "
"search terms people use to find your docs, your most popular pages, and "
"lots of other useful data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:326
msgid ""
"Search term feedback can be used to help you optimize content for certain"
" keywords or for related keywords. For Sphinx documentation, or other "
"technical documentation that has its own search features, analytics tools"
" can also tell you the terms people search for within your site."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/technical-docs-seo-guide.rst:330
msgid ""
"Knowing your popular pages can help you prioritize where to spend your "
"SEO efforts. Optimizing your already popular pages can have a significant"
" impact."
msgstr ""

